-- The eliot repository as an Eliot package: the language's own layers, described in the format the
-- build tool reads. No longer inert — v0.0 is released and eliot.build resolves this file — and it is
-- where this repo's layout stopped being folklore.
--
-- Every layer says `at` — its *package root*, the directory holding `src/` and `compiler/` — because
-- this repository cannot have the layout the convention assumes: it is
-- polyglot and always will be (the compiler is Scala), so Mill owns `lang/src` and the Eliot package
-- sits at `lang/eliot` beside it. The names stay `lang`, `stdlib`, `jvm` — what `//lang` selects is
-- the package, never its directory.
--
-- **No root package**, and none is implied: a package exists exactly where a block declares one, so
-- this file declares no `package root` and a bare `dep` on this repository names nothing. There is no
-- library at the repository root to name — every source here is one layer's, and a consumer selects
-- the layer it wants.
--
-- One asset per package. Each layer ships its compiler plugin as a release asset of this repository at
-- the tag the consumer already selected — no coordinate, no version, no URL, and nothing that can skew
-- from the sources it was built from, because they are the same tag. Each asset holds that package's own
-- jar and the dependencies only it has; nothing is merged, so the per-package service registrations a
-- fat jar would collapse stay separate. `ide/lsp/package.sh` already assembles exactly this shape.
--
-- An `asset` is nothing but a name: every asset in a build's closure goes on the compiler's classpath,
-- and nothing here says which one holds the compiler or which word the backend answers to. The backend
-- registers its own word, and a `compiler` line in a consumer's package either names it (`jvm run`) or
-- leaves it to the one backend accepting the mode (`run`), which is how a platform-independent package
-- can say "run this" at all.
--
-- `lang`'s asset is the one that is not just a layer's own jar: it holds `eliotc`'s too. `eliotc` is
-- the only directory here with no `.els` at all, which makes it the one thing in this repository that
-- cannot be an Eliot package — nothing could ever `dep` it, since a dep line is a statement about
-- sources. Its jar has to ride with a package, and `lang` is the package in every program's closure.
--
-- `apidoc` is in the same position and is deliberately not declared: a backend with no sources of its
-- own, which nothing depends on and which therefore becomes a module the day a configuration names it.

-- The launcher `./eliotw` runs, and the one line of this file the wrapper reads: it greps for the first
-- line starting `launcher `. The launcher itself accepts the line and ignores it.
--
-- Read rather than built: this repository is polyglot and the compiler in it is Scala, so `mill` builds
-- it and always will. What the launcher is for here is seeing these packages the way a *consumer* sees
-- them — `./eliotw roots examples` prints the source roots the layers' `at` clauses resolve to, which is
-- the one check that the descriptor everybody else depends on says what it means to say.
launcher v0.3

package lang {
  at lang/eliot
  asset eliot-compiler.zip
}

package stdlib {
  at stdlib/eliot
  dep //lang
  asset eliot-stdlib.zip
}

package jvm {
  at jvm/eliot
  dep //stdlib
  asset eliot-jvm.zip
}

package examples {
  internal
  dep //jvm
}
